 "section_summary": "This section comprises U.S. diplomatic reporting from the American Embassy in Tokyo and internal State Department memoranda during the summer and autumn of 1939 regarding political, military, and economic developments in Japan and Japanese-occupied China. It contains the concluding portion of the Embassy's monthly report for May 1939 and the major part of the report for June 1939, detailing growing international friction over foreign concessions in Shanghai, Kulangsu, and particularly the Japanese blockade of the British Concession at Tientsin. The documents also record Japanese air bombings of Chinese cities and foreign mission properties, military operations in central and southern China, border clashes with Soviet-Mongolian forces at Nomonhan, and expanding internal economic regimentation under Japan's National Mobilization Law.",
 "subfiles": [
  {
   "about": "Concluding section of the Tokyo Embassy's monthly political and economic report covering May 1939, along with lists of despatches and telegrams sent to Washington.",
   "continues_previous": true,
   "date": "1939-05",
   "doc_type": "report",
   "end_page": 223,
   "from": "American Embassy, Tokyo",
   "keywords": [
    "Shanghai International Settlement",
    "Kulangsu",
    "Chungking bombing",
    "Nomonhan",
    "Commodity Mobilization Plan",
    "Seiyukai",
    "Japanese trade controls"
   ],
   "start_page": 196,
   "summary": "This report details diplomatic disputes involving foreign settlements in China, including British, American, and French protests against Japanese encroachments in Shanghai and the landing of Japanese marines at Kulangsu, Amoy. It reviews widespread Japanese aerial bombardments of Chinese cities, including Chungking, and army operations in Hupeh and Shansi, as well as border clashes between Outer Mongolian and Manchukuo forces at Nomonhan. It also analyzes Japanese internal politics, cabinet decisions on European policy, the adoption of the 1939 Commodity Mobilization Plan, economic control measures, and concludes with comprehensive lists of despatches and telegrams sent to the Department of State during May 1939.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of May, 1939",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "State Department memorandum evaluating the Tokyo Embassy's monthly report for July 1939 and commending its drafter, James Espy.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1939-10-06",
   "doc_type": "memorandum",
   "end_page": 224,
   "from": "Laurence E. Salisbury",
   "keywords": [
    "Far Eastern Affairs",
    "James Espy",
    "Tokyo monthly report",
    "Thailand",
    "Bulgaria",
    "Iraq"
   ],
   "start_page": 224,
   "summary": "Salisbury says the July 1939 report holds nothing new; notes Thai journalists' visit and new legations in Iraq and Bulgaria, and proposes commending James Espy.",
   "title": "Division of Far Eastern Affairs Memorandum on Tokyo's Monthly Report for July 1939",
   "to": "Joseph W. Ballantine"
  },
  {
   "about": "State Department memorandum noting press publicity regarding American protests against Japanese bombings of American property in China mentioned in Tokyo's June 1939 report.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1939-08-14",
   "doc_type": "memorandum",
   "end_page": 225,
   "from": "Laurence E. Salisbury",
   "keywords": [
    "Far Eastern Affairs",
    "Tokyo monthly report",
    "bombing of American property",
    "USS Panay"
   ],
   "start_page": 225,
   "summary": "Salisbury notes the June 1939 report has little new, but mentions press notices of American protests over bombings, the first since the Panay.",
   "title": "Division of Far Eastern Affairs Memorandum on Tokyo's Monthly Report for June 1939",
   "to": "Division of Far Eastern Affairs"
